{"name":"Social Hook","what":"It watches your development work and turns it into social posts in your voice — approved by you, or fully autonomous.","glance":[["Sources","Your actual commits and dev activity"],["Publishes","X and LinkedIn"],["Control","Approval gates in Telegram, web or CLI — or hands-off"],["Engine","Open source on PyPI; B2B SaaS layer in design"]],"archHi":"<p>Social Hook watches your development work and turns it into posts in your voice. The pipeline runs from commit to published post:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Notice.</strong> A hook watches your commits in the background and picks out the ones worth talking about.</li>\n<li><strong>Draft.</strong> An AI composer writes the post in your voice, attaching media when it helps.</li>\n<li><strong>Gate.</strong> Nothing publishes until it clears your approval &mdash; unless you've gone fully autonomous.</li>\n<li><strong>Post.</strong> Live on X and LinkedIn today, with the platform list built to grow.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Your workflow never pauses for any of it: the hook returns before the commit finishes, and drafts wait for you rather than the other way round.</p>","arch":"<p>The commit hook backgrounds itself, so a commit is never blocked; unregistered repositories, off-branch commits and CI environments exit before any model call is made.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>One adapter contract, applied four times.</strong> Publishing platforms, media generators, messaging channels and model providers all implement the same abstract shape &mdash; adding a platform is one factory function and one registration, never a new branch in a dispatch chain.</li>\n<li><strong>Capability flags, not platform names.</strong> Callers branch on what a platform declares it can do; one without threads or native quoting degrades to a link instead of failing.</li>\n<li><strong>Five model providers behind one normalised response type.</strong> The Claude CLI provider embeds its JSON schema in the system prompt rather than using structured-output mode &mdash; the cost is a multi-turn validation loop of three or more round trips.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Between an approved draft and a live post stand four gates, each holding the draft editable rather than failing it:</p>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Gate</th><th>Holds when</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Adapter</td><td>the platform has no adapter registered</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Credentials</td><td>the account isn't connected</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Format</td><td>the draft's format can't be auto-posted</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Media</td><td>referenced media hasn't resolved yet</td></tr>\n</table>","proc":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Python end to end.</strong> A post-commit hook that backgrounds itself, the adapter pipeline behind it, a FastAPI service and a CLI, with releases cut automatically from conventional commits.</li>\n<li><strong>Ports and adapters, applied literally.</strong> Four port families &mdash; publishing platforms, media generators, messaging channels, model providers &mdash; implement one abstract contract. A new platform is a factory function and a registration; nothing in the core knows a platform's name.</li>\n<li><strong>Capability negotiation instead of feature branching.</strong> Callers ask what a platform declares it can do &mdash; threads, native quoting, media &mdash; and degrade gracefully when the answer is no.</li>\n<li><strong>Drafts move through a recoverable state machine.</strong> The four gates between draft and post &mdash; adapter, credentials, format, media &mdash; hold work editable rather than failing it. A blocked draft is a state, not an error.</li>\n</ul>","loCap":"Under the hood — the working architecture.","diagram":"<svg id=\"social-hook-light-my-svg\" width=\"100%\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\" class=\"flowchart\" style=\"max-width: 583.164px; background-color: transparent;\" viewBox=\"0 0 583.1640625 677.71875\" role=\"graphics-document document\" aria-roledescription=\"flowchart-v2\"><style>#socia